From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU interpreter: cheaper FMAC common path, zero results on the fast path, in-place immediate writes

What it is for:
  After patch 0033 a translated four-lane MADD still cost about 280 host
  instructions, and the cross-product pair OPMULA/OPMSUB took the exact
  model on every call (their zero components give exact zero results).

The change (same results):
  - fmacProductOperands4: the product operands of MADD/MSUB/OPMSUB (and
    of the plain products MUL/MULA/OPMULA) for all lanes, from operands
    normalised four lanes at a time, instead of a per-lane reader with a
    branchy normalisation per operand.
  - applyFmacSlow: normalizeFmacResult + calculateFmacProductSticky +
    updateFmacFlags out of line (PS2_VU_NOINLINE), compiled once instead
    of into every translated upper word; the common path keeps few saved
    registers.
  - fmacFlagsFast also accepts a lane whose result is a signed zero when
    a product operand is zero (product-sum and plain-product words): the
    exact product is then a signed zero, so the exact result is that same
    signed zero and the exact model gives Z plus its sign. Plain products
    read their operands only when a lane is outside the plain range.
  - execPairWith with immediate writes (translated code): a VF/ACC write
    the executors already made in place is not saved and restored (the
    executors write only the dest lanes); only the write sequence and the
    pending mark are kept (noteVfWriteNow/noteAccWriteNow). Not when the
    lower instruction writes the upper's register.
  - recordViWriteForBranch inline.

How it was checked:
  The port's replay: same checksums and cycle counts on the 40 VU1 and 60
  VU0 workloads, interpreted and translated; translated VU work 26.4 M ->
  24.4 M host instructions (excluding the replay's code resolution).
  ps2xTest 454/454. A throwaway randomised comparison against the
  per-lane model (2 million FMAC operations over every opcode and dest
  mask, with signed-zero operands and accumulators; operand readers per
  lane; applyFmacDest/applyFmacDestAcc end to end) found no difference,
  plain and with -march=x86-64-v3 -ffp-contract=fast.

